@node Needs
@chapter Needs

Bravais boots to a TTY through greetd/tuigreet. Between the firmware logo and the greeter the machine shows kernel and systemd text, or a Plymouth theme --- a C daemon running as root in the initrd, whose progress bar is a guess based on the previous boot. The maintainer wants the boot path free of that C code, the bar to report what systemd is actually doing, and the whole thing themed from the same Steelbore palette and Nickel tooling as the rest of the OS.

The operating environment is a NixOS flake (Bravais) with @code{boot.initrd.systemd}, UEFI with simpledrm, LUKS-encrypted roots, and Intel/AMD KMS drivers. The constraints the maintainer will not trade away: Rust with @code{unsafe} confined to qualified dependencies; the splash can never be the reason a machine fails to boot; and Steelbore Standard §18 accessibility is a shipped mode, not a later patch.

The reference machine (Steelbore Standard §22.4) is the maintainer's ThinkPad T490s: Intel i915 graphics, NVMe storage, a 1920x1080 panel, running Bravais with a LUKS root. The reference workload is a cold boot from power-on to greetd with the reference theme and a 24-frame animation. Wherever a requirement says "on the reference machine" it means this machine and this workload.

@anchor{FRN-NEED-001}
@subheading FRN-NEED-001 --- A splash from the first drawable frame until greetd owns the VT
@cindex FRN-NEED-001
A graphical splash from the earliest point the initrd can draw until greetd owns the VT, with no visible console text in between on a healthy boot.

@noindent
Stakeholder: Maintainer as user.

@anchor{FRN-NEED-002}
@subheading FRN-NEED-002 --- A progress bar that reflects real boot work
@cindex FRN-NEED-002
A progress bar whose value reflects real boot work rather than elapsed time.

@noindent
Stakeholder: Maintainer as user.

@anchor{FRN-NEED-003}
@subheading FRN-NEED-003 --- The LUKS passphrase prompt drawn inside the splash
@cindex FRN-NEED-003
The LUKS passphrase prompt drawn inside the splash, so an encrypted boot looks no different from an unencrypted one.

@noindent
Stakeholder: Maintainer as user.

@anchor{FRN-NEED-004}
@subheading FRN-NEED-004 --- Theming expressed in Nickel
@cindex FRN-NEED-004
Theming --- palette, logo, layout, animation --- expressed in Nickel so a flavour can ship its own theme without rebuilding Fairing.

@noindent
Stakeholder: Maintainer as OS integrator.

@anchor{FRN-NEED-005}
@subheading FRN-NEED-005 --- A way to see the boot, and failures never hidden
@cindex FRN-NEED-005
A way to see what the boot is doing (Esc) and a guarantee that failures are never hidden behind the splash.

@noindent
Stakeholder: Maintainer as operator.

@anchor{FRN-NEED-006}
@subheading FRN-NEED-006 --- An accessible boot
@cindex FRN-NEED-006
A text-only accessible mode, reduced motion, and a high-contrast palette, so the boot is usable with a screen reader attached to the console or with low vision.

@noindent
Stakeholder: Maintainer; Steelbore Standard §18.

